      {
        "id": "s3",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Perpendicular lines: the negative reciprocal",
        "body": "Two lines at right angles have gradients that **multiply to $-1$**. Flip the fraction and change the sign.\n\n| Gradient | Perpendicular |\n|---|---|\n| $2$ | $-\\tfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| $-\\tfrac{1}{3}$ | $3$ |\n| $\\tfrac{3}{4}$ | $-\\tfrac{4}{3}$ |\n\nCheck: $2 \\times \\left(-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1$.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "component": "line-graph",
            "props": {
              "xRange": [
                -5,
                5
              ],
              "yRange": [
                -5,
                5
              ],
              "lines": [
                {
                  "m": 2,
                  "c": 0,
                  "label": "y = 2x"
                },
                {
                  "m": -0.5,
                  "c": 0,
                  "label": "y = −½x"
                }
              ]
            },
            "alt": "The lines y equals 2x and y equals minus a half x crossing at right angles at the origin"
          },
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to $y = 3x - 1$ that passes through $(6, 2)$.",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "The given gradient is $3$, so the perpendicular gradient is $-\\tfrac{1}{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = -\\tfrac{1}{3}x + c$. Substitute $(6, 2)$: $2 = -2 + c$, so $c = 4$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$y = -\\tfrac{1}{3}x + 4$, which can also be written $x + 3y = 12$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
          "id": "c3",
          "type": "numeric",
          "prompt": "Write down the gradient of a line perpendicular to a line with gradient $4$.",
          "marks": 1,
          "gradeBand": "4-5",
          "skill": "perpendicular gradients",
          "calculator": "either",
          "tags": [],
          "solution": "$-\\tfrac{1}{4}$, because $4 \\times \\left(-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\right) = -1$.",
          "markScheme": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
