      {
        "id": "s5",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Rearrange first",
        "body": "You cannot compare $2x + 3y = 6$ with anything until it is $y = mx + c$. $3y = -2x + 6$, so $y = -\\tfrac{2}{3}x + 2$: gradient $-\\tfrac{2}{3}$. A line perpendicular to it has gradient $\\tfrac{3}{2}$.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Are $2x + 3y = 6$ and $3x - 2y = 4$ perpendicular?",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "First: $y = -\\tfrac{2}{3}x + 2$, gradient $-\\tfrac{2}{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "Second: $2y = 3x - 4$, $y = \\tfrac{3}{2}x - 2$, gradient $\\tfrac{3}{2}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$-\\tfrac{2}{3} \\times \\tfrac{3}{2} = -1$. Yes."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
